\begin{align*}
\mathbf{M}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} (-2)(4) & (-2)(-5)+(0)(4) \\ (-5)(4)+(-2)(0) & (-5)(-5)+(0)(4) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 8 & 10 \\ -20 & 25 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
Now, we want to find scalars $p$ and $q$ such that
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{M}^2 &= p\mathbf{M}+q\mathbf{I} \\
\begin{pmatrix} 8 & 10 \\ -20 & 25 \end{pmatrix} &= p\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix}+q\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
\begin{pmatrix} 8 & 10 \\ -20 & 25 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} 0p-5q & -5p \\ -2p+4q & 4p+4q \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
Comparing the entries, we have the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
8 &= 0p-5q \\
-20 &= -5p \\
25 &= -2p+4q \\
10 &= 5p
\end{align*}
Solving this system, we find that $p=1$ and $q=1$. Therefore, the ordered pair $(p,q)$ is $\boxed{(1,1)}$.

