To find $p$ and $q$, we need to calculate $\mathbf{M}^2$ and then find the values of $p$ and $q$ such that $\mathbf{M}^2 = p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I}$.

\begin{align*}
\mathbf{M}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & (-5)(-2) \\ (-2)(0) & (-2)(4) + (-5)(4) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 10 \\ 0 & -14 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

We can now solve the equation $\mathbf{M}^2 = p \mathbf{M} + q \mathbf{I}$ for $p$ and $q$.

\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 10 \\ 0 & -14 \end{pmatrix} &= p \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} + q \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 10 \\ 0 & -14 \end{pmatrix} &= \begin{pmatrix} -5p & -5p + 4q \\ -2p + 4q & 4p + 4q \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

Equating entries gives the following system of equations:
\begin{align*}
0 &= -5p \\
0 &= -5p + 4q \\
10 &= -2p + 4q \\
-14 &= 4p + 4q
\end{align*}

From the first equation, we have $p=0$. Substituting $p=0$ into the second equation, we have $0=-5(0)+4q$, so $q=10/4=\frac{5}{2}$. Thus, the ordered pair $(p,q)=\boxed{(0,5/2)}$.

