\begin{align*}
\frac{d}{dt}x &= \frac{d}{dt}(t^2+t) \\
\frac{d}{dt}(t^2+t) &= 2t+1 \\
2t+1 &= 0 \\
t &= -\frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}Using the value of $t$ found above, we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by substituting $t=-\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation for $x$:
\begin{align*}
x &= t^2+t \\
