\mathbf{M}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -5 \\ -2 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} (-2)(4) & (-2)(-5)+(0)(4) \\ (-2)(-5)+(-2)(4) & (-2)(4)+(0)(4) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 8 & 10 \\ 10 & 8 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
Now we want to find scalars $p$ and $q$ such that $p\mathbf{M}+\mathbf{I}$ equals the above matrix.
\begin{align*}
p\mathbf{M}+\mathbf{I} &= \begin{pmatrix} p(0) & p(-5) \\ p(-2) & p(4) \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} p(-5) & p(4) \\ p(-2) & p(4)+1 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} p(-5)+1 & p(4) \\ p(-2) & p(4)+1+1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} p(-5)+1 & p(4) \\ p(-2) & p(4)+2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
Comparing the two matrices, we see that we need $p(-5)+1=8$ and $p(4)+2=8$.
From the first equation, we have $p=-\frac{3}{5}$.
From the second equation, we have $p=-\frac{1}{2}$.
Since we want the smallest possible $p$, we take

\begin{align*}
\dbinom{1001}{2} &= \frac{1001\cdot 999}{2} \\
&= 500500 \\
&= 2\cdot 5\cdot 5^3\cdot 3\cdot 7 \\
&= 2\cdot 5^4\cdot 3\cdot 7.
\end{align*}
Thus, $\dbinom{1001}{2}=\boxed{2\cdot 5^4\cdot 3\cdot 7}$.

